WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.070
Comprehension.

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:06.620
Comprehension, as I've said before, is
the key to success in language learning.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:08.790
It's not difficult to say something.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.639
It's not difficult to come up
with a sentence, a limited meaning

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:13.979
that you want to get across.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.610
But when you're speaking to a native
speaker who has tens of thousands of

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:22.169
words that they potentially could use,
many different ways of expressing the

00:00:22.169 --> 00:00:26.070
same thought or responding to what
you have said, if you haven't got

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.619
good comprehension, you're not going
to be in the conversation very long.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.940
You can struggle to get
your own meaning across, but you

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.460
have to have good comprehension.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.390
How do you get good comprehension?

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:41.120
I think there are five concepts that
I haven't felt in my own experience

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.419
have been very useful to me.

00:00:42.759 --> 00:00:48.409
The first one is we have to get rid of
the old idea that we have to know 95

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.739
percent of the words in any given context
in order to be able to benefit from that.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:58.364
Well, first of all, I've found
that even 5% of the words, if

00:00:58.364 --> 00:01:01.635
I don't know those
words, it's frustrating to read.

00:01:01.764 --> 00:01:04.695
And in fact, that's what got me
going into LingQ is I had books in

00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:08.595
Spanish and German with on every
page, you know, 10 words that I didn't

00:01:08.595 --> 00:01:12.035
know, and I would look them up in a
dictionary and I would forget them.

00:01:12.075 --> 00:01:15.294
So that's not that comfortable
in fact, although you get a

00:01:15.294 --> 00:01:16.565
general sense of the meaning.

00:01:16.685 --> 00:01:21.115
However, nowadays with online
dictionaries and with systems like

00:01:21.125 --> 00:01:24.735
LingQ, I personally find that 10 to 15%.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.860
Unknown words is like a sweet spot
where I'm gaining more vocabulary,

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.550
because if you are hoping to improve
your comprehension and improve your

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.239
vocabulary, if you're only dealing with
texts that have 5 percent new words,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:43.490
you will take forever to increase your
vocabulary to a level where you can be

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.980
comfortable in a wide variety of contexts.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:50.030
So first of all, with modern technology,
push yourself a little bit, take

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:54.060
advantage of online dictionaries,
take advantage of systems like LingQ.

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:56.370
And find your own sweet spot.

00:01:56.539 --> 00:01:59.830
Some people might, if the subject
is very interesting, might even

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.700
accept that 20 percent unknown words.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:06.860
I find that tougher, but 10 to 15
percent for me is quite comfortable.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.060
Second of all, traditionally, if
you look at teachers approaches to

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.130
comprehension, reading comprehension,
reading strategies, you'll see

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.240
this, for example, Scarborough's
Rope, which talks about the various

00:02:20.575 --> 00:02:22.065
aspects of comprehension.

00:02:22.165 --> 00:02:25.925
To me, it's an unnecessary complication of
something that's pretty straightforward.

00:02:26.075 --> 00:02:29.955
But the gist of it is that we
need prior knowledge or background

00:02:29.955 --> 00:02:31.975
knowledge, as well as vocabulary.

00:02:32.114 --> 00:02:32.885
And this is true.

00:02:32.895 --> 00:02:37.885
Research has shown that front loading
vocabulary, in other words, trying

00:02:37.885 --> 00:02:41.815
to teach, say a list of words that
the reader or the listener is about

00:02:41.815 --> 00:02:46.345
to encounter is not as effective
as having content that the reader.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:51.400
The listener is already familiar
with prior knowledge, so how do you

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:57.440
ensure that the reader can encounter
material that's familiar to them?

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:01.640
I think one of the issues there is to let
the learner, the language learner, choose

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.275
content that they want to learn about.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.270
They have to like that content.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:09.180
Traditionally, teachers like to come
up with all kinds of pre reading

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:14.829
strategies, asking questions and helping
the learner, the reader infer or guess.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.790
And I think all of that is
a complete waste of time.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.469
Rather, you can have the learner
listen to something in a classroom

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:26.230
that kind of primes the pump a little
bit, or you can let the learner.

00:03:26.575 --> 00:03:29.624
Choose the content that they want
to learn from, in which case the

00:03:29.624 --> 00:03:32.415
learner will probably choose something
that they're already familiar with.

00:03:32.635 --> 00:03:36.524
It's interesting reading some of these
teacher's manuals about what to do.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:41.964
They point out, for example, that person
of Indian origin say will find content

00:03:41.964 --> 00:03:47.474
about something of Indian culture easier
to understand if they're learning English.

00:03:47.644 --> 00:03:48.839
That's obviously true.

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:52.799
But that being the case, why is it so
often that foreign language teachers

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:57.319
basically try to force something that is
culturally very different, but connected

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:01.219
to the culture of the target language,
onto the learner at an early stage?

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.259
All of these things you
can deal with later on.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.389
The main thing is to get the learner
engaged with the content, and in

00:04:08.389 --> 00:04:11.859
that sense, Familiar subjects,
things they already have prior

00:04:11.859 --> 00:04:14.660
knowledge about is far preferable.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.809
Also, uh, in going through the sort
of various Google searches, teachers,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:23.469
when they get into this sort of
pre reading, they end up trying to

00:04:23.479 --> 00:04:28.009
direct the reader, at least in high
school classroom, wanting to make

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:30.699
sure that the reader or the learner.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.739
Uh, even in sort of English as a second
language class draws the same inference.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.690
So, you know, the inference
that aligns with the ideological

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.590
persuasion of the teacher, there's
a time and a place for everything.

00:04:41.599 --> 00:04:45.010
And if the teacher is more interested
in promoting their ideology,

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:48.829
whether it be left wing or right
wing, rather than just letting the.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.700
Reader or the learner absorb the
language and draw their own reference,

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.530
even if that inference is wrong.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:59.000
It doesn't matter that you know,
interpretation of the text belongs

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.260
to the reader or the learner.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:04.790
The target is to let the learner
or the reader acquire words and

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:08.810
acquire some familiarity with
the language, some fluency, and.

00:05:09.645 --> 00:05:14.344
So prior knowledge, yes, but
obviously prior knowledge can only

00:05:14.344 --> 00:05:15.934
be acquired through a lot of reading.

00:05:15.945 --> 00:05:20.755
So the, the sort of bottom line here
is do things that will enable or

00:05:20.755 --> 00:05:24.184
encourage the reader to read a lot.

00:05:24.455 --> 00:05:29.655
And that brings me to the third point, and
that is we shouldn't be, again, giving the

00:05:29.665 --> 00:05:34.465
learner comprehension questions because
we should be encouraging the reader

00:05:34.515 --> 00:05:38.025
to accept, or the learner, to accept
the fact that they won't understand.

00:05:38.225 --> 00:05:42.515
That a lot of what they listen to or
read in a language they're learning,

00:05:42.805 --> 00:05:43.855
they're not going to understand it.

00:05:44.275 --> 00:05:47.375
I sometimes call this, get
it wrong the first time.

00:05:47.665 --> 00:05:51.334
When I worked in the lumber business, we
had this quality control program where

00:05:51.335 --> 00:05:52.815
they said, get it right the first time.

00:05:52.824 --> 00:05:54.965
Well, if you're manufacturing,
get it right the first time.

00:05:55.225 --> 00:05:57.725
But if you're a language learner,
get it wrong the first time.

00:05:57.955 --> 00:05:58.855
Keep going.

00:05:59.105 --> 00:06:00.415
You can always come back to it.

00:06:00.495 --> 00:06:04.495
You know, I also used once this
sort of lawnmower analogy where.

00:06:04.919 --> 00:06:09.130
If I'm mowing the lawn and I've let the
lawn grow too long, I'm better off to,

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.679
you know, raise the level of my knives
so that I can go across the grass, you

00:06:14.679 --> 00:06:19.489
know, cutting some of it and then come
back later on and take a second pass.

00:06:19.559 --> 00:06:20.799
Make it easier for yourself.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:24.559
Take a first pass, get a lot of stuff
wrong, maybe go off and do something

00:06:24.559 --> 00:06:28.499
else, come back again, have a second
pass and pick up a little bit more.

00:06:28.539 --> 00:06:31.739
We need to accept the fact that we can
get it wrong, we cannot understand it.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.690
We don't have to answer
comprehension questions, which

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:35.970
I find extremely annoying.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:40.950
It's enough that I have consumed a
lot of words, consumed a lot of the

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:44.829
language, and all of that is helping
my brain get used to the language.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.050
You also want to sort
of come across these

00:06:48.065 --> 00:06:52.735
Vocabulary items, words, expressions,
phrases in different contexts.

00:06:52.755 --> 00:06:56.555
It's also been proven that more than
the sort of frequency, the sort of

00:06:56.585 --> 00:07:00.324
repetition of the same items, which
is sometimes the case of people who,

00:07:00.555 --> 00:07:04.975
who study lists, blocks, flashcards,
whatever, what's more important is.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.800
To encounter the same words in
a variety of different contexts.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:13.389
And that way, if it turns out the
brain is able to better retain them.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:16.360
So several passes over the same content.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:20.210
Be prepared not to understand,
just keep consuming the language.

00:07:20.525 --> 00:07:24.615
However, and this is the fourth point,
you want to make it easy on yourself.

00:07:24.645 --> 00:07:29.975
So it's, it is useful to try to
narrow the range of sort of subject

00:07:29.975 --> 00:07:31.165
matter that you're dealing with.

00:07:31.495 --> 00:07:35.964
And I tend to do this if I follow a
podcast, you know, on politics in say

00:07:35.964 --> 00:07:41.375
Arabic or economics, or if I can narrow
the range of subject matter that I'm

00:07:41.375 --> 00:07:45.265
dealing with, even if I'm dealing with
different, like I'm interleaving in the

00:07:45.265 --> 00:07:46.715
sense that I'm dealing with different.

00:07:47.225 --> 00:07:51.805
Podcast episodes or different sources
of content, but it's in a somewhat

00:07:51.805 --> 00:07:54.065
narrower range of subject matter.

00:07:54.115 --> 00:07:58.385
I'll have a higher probability of meeting
the same vocabulary over and over again.

00:07:58.685 --> 00:08:01.114
I have then some familiarity
with the subject.

00:08:01.485 --> 00:08:03.104
I have that prior knowledge.

00:08:03.454 --> 00:08:08.565
If I move away, for example, from a
Political podcast to say literature or

00:08:08.565 --> 00:08:13.075
something on an unrelated subject, having
to do with, you know, dating or something,

00:08:13.355 --> 00:08:14.745
then it's going to be much more difficult.

00:08:14.755 --> 00:08:19.385
So stay within the same sort
of general range of content.

00:08:19.395 --> 00:08:21.565
And that way you're going
to achieve that goal of.

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:26.520
Uh, more familiar vocabulary and
some sense of prior knowledge.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.830
And finally, a fifth point is use
the power of listening because while

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.490
reading, as I've said before, is a
great way to increase your vocabulary.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.989
You can look words up
in an online dictionary.

00:08:37.989 --> 00:08:39.329
You can review them.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.070
Um, but the listening is how
I get into the subject matter.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:44.830
Typically I listen first.

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:45.840
I'm curious.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.889
Now I've got some sense
of what it's about.

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:52.300
I've got a lot of missing pieces that
I can go in and read and, uh, look up

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:55.870
words and get a better sense of what's
there, but the thing about listening is

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:59.870
that listening goes faster in most cases,
depending on where we are in the language.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:03.040
But certainly for a long
time, the speed at which.

00:09:03.365 --> 00:09:07.005
The narrator, native speaker is
speaking is faster than the speed at

00:09:07.005 --> 00:09:08.535
which we can read in the language.

00:09:08.584 --> 00:09:11.805
And so that means we're
getting a better, faster.

00:09:12.114 --> 00:09:15.314
Sort of access to the content
that we're trying to read.

00:09:15.314 --> 00:09:17.254
We have some momentum
when we start to read.

00:09:17.354 --> 00:09:21.474
And very often, even if, if the
narrator is speaking too slowly,

00:09:21.574 --> 00:09:22.994
in fact, it's a disadvantage.

00:09:22.994 --> 00:09:26.305
We start to lose track by the time
we come to the end of the sentence

00:09:26.305 --> 00:09:28.994
of what he or she was saying at
the beginning of the sentence.

00:09:29.074 --> 00:09:32.155
So don't listen to material too slowly.

00:09:32.425 --> 00:09:35.425
Try and force yourself to listen
to material at normal speed.

00:09:35.574 --> 00:09:39.004
In fact, I sometimes crank
up the speed at LingQ.

00:09:39.225 --> 00:09:42.125
In order to listen to it more quickly,
I can cover the material more quickly.

00:09:42.185 --> 00:09:45.694
And I find I get a, in some ways
a better, almost like a bird's eye

00:09:45.704 --> 00:09:50.005
view of what I'm listening to by the
mere fact that it's going faster.

00:09:50.095 --> 00:09:54.945
And in fact, I did mention in a previous
video that There was this dyslexic

00:09:55.005 --> 00:10:00.065
person, whose name now escapes me, who
developed a methodology of, you know,

00:10:00.125 --> 00:10:04.675
listening at two or three times normal
speed in order to overcome his dyslexia.

00:10:04.835 --> 00:10:09.794
So there is some advantage in forcing
yourself to listen and to listen quickly.

00:10:09.804 --> 00:10:14.914
And of course, to listen a lot, to build
up your momentum and your comprehension

00:10:14.914 --> 00:10:16.444
skills for when you go into reading.

00:10:16.804 --> 00:10:17.914
So in summary.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:23.880
I've given you five strategies for
improved comprehension, both reading

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.569
comprehension and listening comprehension.

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:26.839
This is the key.

00:10:26.849 --> 00:10:30.780
This is the base for your
progress in the language.

00:10:30.999 --> 00:10:35.489
Once you have good comprehension, you
can work on refining bits of grammar.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.050
You can work on improving your output, but
the key is to get that good comprehension.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:44.720
And one important element is for
you to believe in yourself and

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:46.120
for you to trust the process.

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:49.330
If you keep on doing these things, if
you keep on consuming the language,

00:10:49.549 --> 00:10:50.950
you will achieve your goals.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:55.670
So, thank you for listening, and I have
spoken before on comprehension, so you

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.300
might want to check out these other
videos that I did on the same subject.

00:10:59.619 --> 00:11:00.310
Bye for now.

